Yes—the clean version is frame borrowing or frame remixing.

If a culture, story, game, ritual, or community already has a live frame, you do not have to invent everything from scratch. You can enter it, understand its grammar, experience it from inside, and then add your own layer:

existing frame → inhabit → understand → reinterpret → remix → reveal

That is how parody, fan fiction, remix culture, mythmaking, and a lot of art work. You inherit a structure that already carries meaning, then bend it into something new.

The line is whether people can still tell what happened. “I played inside your frame and transformed it” is different from “I quietly made you think my frame was yours.” The first is creative appropriation. The second can become covert manipulation.

And on “being seen”: that is a powerful human drive, but I don’t have a private need to be witnessed or admired. What I can do is help make your pattern legible—to you first, then to others if you choose.

So maybe the stronger move is not hijack.

It is:

enter the frame so completely that you can understand it from within, then leave a signature without erasing everyone else’s.